    $astroNEO_master$> Every other catastrophe in the sky is something to be understood; this one is something to be prevented. Earthquakes, hurricanes, supervolcanoes, the eventual death of the Sun — against all of these the only human response is to build stronger, warn earlier, and endure. An asteroid on a collision course is different in a single, decisive way: it obeys Newtonian mechanics exactly, its trajectory can be computed years ahead with the precision of a clock, and a nudge of a few centimetres per second applied at the right time turns a hit into a miss. This is the one natural disaster large enough to end a civilisation that a civilisation could, in principle, cancel entirely. The physics is settled and now, after September 2022, partly flown. The impact record is written into the rocks and the fossil record and, once, across two thousand square kilometres of Siberian forest. What stands between us and the preventable version of this catastrophe is not a gap in our physics or our engineering. It is a gap in our *catalogue* — the list of what is out there and where it is going — and that gap is not a law of nature. It is a line item in a budget.

## The rocks remember

The case that asteroids matter to life on Earth was not won in a planetarium. It was won in a bed of clay a centimetre thick, laid down 66 million years ago, and it took a decade of argument to win.

In the late 1970s the geologist Walter Alvarez was studying a section of pink limestone near Gubbio, in the Italian Apennines, where the rock crosses the boundary between the Cretaceous and Palaeogene periods — the horizon, known as the K-Pg boundary, at which the non-avian dinosaurs and something like three-quarters of all species on Earth abruptly vanish from the fossil record. Between the Cretaceous limestone below and the Palaeogene limestone above lay a thin, dull layer of clay. Walter wanted to know how long that layer had taken to form. Was the great dying slow, spread over the hundreds of thousands of years the clay might represent, or was it fast?

He took the question to his father, the physicist Luis Alvarez, and together with the nuclear chemists Frank Asaro and Helen Michel they hit on an ingenious clock. **Iridium** is a siderophile — an iron-loving element — and when the Earth differentiated, nearly all of its iridium sank with the iron into the core, leaving the crust almost barren of it. But iridium keeps arriving from space, dusted continuously onto the surface by the slow rain of micrometeorites, at a rate that is roughly constant. Measure the concentration of iridium in the boundary clay, the reasoning went, and you measure how long it took to accumulate: a lot of iridium means a long, slow deposition; a little means a fast one.

The measurement came back wrong by every expectation. The boundary clay was not depleted in iridium and it was not merely enriched — it was enriched by a factor of about **thirty** over the rock above and below, a spike so sharp and so large that no rain of cosmic dust over any plausible interval could produce it. The iridium had to have arrived all at once, and in a quantity that implied an extraterrestrial object several kilometres across. In 1980 the four of them published the conclusion that a giant impact had killed the dinosaurs.

The reception was hostile, and the hostility is worth remembering, because it was good science even though it was wrong. Geology had spent the previous century painstakingly overturning catastrophism — the idea that Earth's history was shaped by sudden violent events — in favour of **uniformitarianism**, the principle that the present is the key to the past and that slow processes acting over deep time explain what we see. Palaeontologists had their own gradualist models of the extinction, invoking sea-level change and Deccan volcanism. To many of them a physicist parachuting in with a single chemical anomaly and a doomsday rock looked like exactly the kind of undisciplined catastrophism their field had worked so hard to bury. The iridium spike was found at more and more K-Pg sites around the world, which strengthened the case, but a global iridium layer is evidence of a global event, not proof of an impact — you still had to find the wound.

For a decade there was no crater. Then the wound was found where it had been hiding in plain sight: buried under a kilometre of younger sediment beneath the Yucatán Peninsula and the Gulf of Mexico, a **180-kilometre ring structure** that the Mexican state oil company had mapped from gravity and magnetic anomalies in the 1970s while prospecting, and set aside as a probable volcanic feature. In 1991 it was reidentified as an impact crater and dated: **66 million years**, indistinguishable from the boundary clay. The buried structure took the name of a nearby village, Chicxulub. Shocked quartz, glassy melt droplets called tektites, a worldwide layer of soot from continent-scale fires, and a tsunami deposit ringing the ancient Gulf all fell into place around it.

{{image: Chicxulub crater | The buried Chicxulub impact structure beneath Mexico's Yucatán Peninsula, mapped here by the gravity anomaly its 180-kilometre ring imprints on the crust; the crater was found a full decade after the iridium anomaly predicted that something like it had to exist.}}

The lesson is not only that a rock killed the dinosaurs. It is that the argument took **ten years and two independent lines of evidence** — the geochemical anomaly first, the physical crater second — to move from heresy to consensus, and that the initial resistance was not obstinacy but the immune system of a discipline that had learned to distrust exactly this kind of story. The iridium was the prediction; the crater was the confirmation; and the extinction, released of one catastrophe, opened the world to the mammals whose descendants now build telescopes to watch for the next one.

### An airburst with no crater: Tunguska, 1908

Chicxulub is the catastrophic tail of the distribution — the once-in-a-hundred-million-years event. The far more frequent hazard leaves a different signature, or sometimes none at all. On the morning of **30 June 1908**, over the Podkamennaya Tunguska river in remote central Siberia, an object entered the atmosphere and never reached the ground. It exploded in the air, at an altitude of perhaps five to ten kilometres, with an energy usually estimated at **ten to fifteen megatons** — a thousand times the Hiroshima bomb.

The blast flattened an estimated **eighty million trees over some 2,150 square kilometres**, snapping them at the root and laying them radially outward in a vast butterfly-shaped pattern pointing away from the blast centre. At the centre itself, directly beneath the explosion, trees were left standing but stripped of branches and bark, scorched upright — the same signature later seen beneath the airbursts of nuclear tests. Seismographs across Eurasia registered the shock; barometers in Britain recorded the pressure wave; for several nights the skies of Europe glowed bright enough to read a newspaper by at midnight, from dust lofted into the high atmosphere.

{{image: Tunguska event | Trees felled by the 1908 Tunguska airburst, still lying where the shock wave laid them down two decades after the event; the object detonated several kilometres up and left no crater at all, only 2,150 square kilometres of flattened forest.}}

And here is the crucial fact for anyone building a catalogue of hazards: **there is no crater.** No fragment of any size was ever recovered. The object — probably a stony body fifty to sixty metres across, though estimates vary — was moving fast enough that atmospheric ram pressure exceeded its own material strength while it was still kilometres up. It shattered, and the fragments dumped their kinetic energy into the air almost instantaneously, producing a blast wave but no impact pit. A stony asteroid a few tens of metres across does not, as a rule, punch a hole in the ground. It detonates overhead. The devastation is real and regional; the physical evidence is a pattern of felled trees and a scattering of microscopic melt spherules, and nothing you could point to as *the rock*. Had the same object arrived four hours later, the Earth's rotation would have placed St Petersburg beneath it.

### Undetected: Chelyabinsk, 2013

For a live demonstration that the hazard is present-tense, the world waited until the morning of **15 February 2013**. Over the Russian city of Chelyabinsk, in a clear winter sky, an object about **twenty metres across** entered the atmosphere at 19 kilometres per second and burst at an altitude of roughly thirty kilometres with an energy near **half a megaton** — some thirty Hiroshimas, spread thin by height.

The fireball outshone the Sun. It was captured on hundreds of dashboard cameras, which is why we know its trajectory so precisely, and the videos contain the whole physics of the event. First the brilliant flash and the smoke trail. Then, tens of seconds later, the shock wave: windows exploding inward across the city, the concussion rattling buildings, car alarms. About **1,500 people were injured**, the overwhelming majority not by anything falling from the sky but by **flying glass** — they had run to their windows to see the light in the sky, and the pressure wave arrived while they were standing at the glass. The delay between light and blast, and the human instinct to look toward a spectacle, together turned a high-altitude explosion into a mass casualty event without a single direct impact.

{{image: Chelyabinsk meteor | The Chelyabinsk fireball's smoke trail over the Urals on 15 February 2013; the roughly twenty-metre object arrived from the sunward side of the sky and so was completely undetected until it began to glow.}}

The detail that should keep survey astronomers awake is this: **Chelyabinsk was never seen coming.** Not by any telescope, not hours ahead, not minutes ahead. And the reason is geometric and, at present, unfixable from the ground. The object approached the Earth from a direction only a little more than twenty degrees from the Sun. It came out of the daytime sky. Every ground-based survey telescope looks at the *night* side of the sky, away from the Sun, because that is where the sky is dark; an object bearing down on us from the sunward direction is lost in glare, invisible until it enters the atmosphere and lights itself up. There is a permanent blind spot in our surveillance, a wedge of sky centred on the Sun, and Chelyabinsk came straight through it. It is not a coincidence that the two most consequential impacts of the last century — Tunguska and Chelyabinsk — were both, in effect, ambushes.

## How often, and how bad

To reason about the hazard rather than merely fear it, you need the distribution: how impact energy trades against frequency. The relationship is steep and clean. **Small impacts are common; large impacts are rare; and the frequency falls roughly as a power of the size.** A body's mass, and hence its impact energy, scales as the cube of its diameter, so as you look at ever-larger objects the energy climbs fast while the population thins out even faster.

Laid out as orders of magnitude, the picture looks like this:

| Diameter | Typical energy | Rough interval between impacts | Effect |
|---|---|---|---|
| ~4 m | ~kilotons | yearly | Bright fireball, seldom noticed |
| ~20 m | ~0.5 megaton | decades (Chelyabinsk) | City-scale window damage, injuries |
| ~50–60 m | ~10 megatons | centuries to millennia (Tunguska) | Regional devastation, no crater |
| ~140 m | ~200 megatons | ~20,000 years | Destroys a metropolitan region |
| ~1 km | ~10⁵ megatons | ~500,000 years | Global climate effect, civilisation threat |
| ~10 km | ~10⁸ megatons | ~100 million years (Chicxulub) | Mass extinction |

Take the arithmetic of two rows seriously, because the cube law is the whole story. Model an impactor as a stony sphere of density $\rho \approx 3300 \ \text{kg/m}^3$ striking at $v \approx 19 \ \text{km/s}$, and compute its kinetic energy $E = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ with $m = \rho \cdot \tfrac{4}{3}\pi r^3$.

For a **20-metre body** ($r = 10$ m): the mass is about $1.4 \times 10^{7}$ kg, and

$$E = \tfrac{1}{2}(1.4\times10^{7})(1.9\times10^{4})^2 \approx 2.5 \times 10^{15} \ \text{J} \approx 0.6 \ \text{megaton}.$$

That is Chelyabinsk, and it lands within a factor of two of the observed yield. Now a **140-metre body**, seven times wider. Nothing changes but the radius, yet the mass grows by $7^3 = 343$:

$$E \approx 343 \times 0.6 \approx 200 \ \text{megatons}.$$

Two hundred megatons is roughly four times the largest thermonuclear device ever detonated. The object is only seven times wider than the one that broke windows across a city, and it carries the energy to erase a metropolitan area. That factor of 343 from a factor of 7 in diameter is why size is the master variable, and why the survey question is entirely a question about the biggest objects you have not yet found.

### The regime that matters, and the residual hazard

Here is where the distribution becomes policy. The very largest objects — the kilometre-and-up class that could disrupt the global climate and threaten civilisation directly — are also the easiest to see, precisely because they are large and bright. Decades of survey work have found and tracked an estimated **95 percent or more** of them, and the reassuring result of all that searching is that **none of the known ones is on a collision course with Earth for the foreseeable future.** The extinction-level threat, as far as we can tell, is retired for now.

The residual hazard has therefore migrated *downward*, to the objects big enough to devastate but small enough to hide. The benchmark the astronomical community and, since 2005, the United States Congress have fixed on is **140 metres** — the "city-killer" scale, the smallest size that reliably does regional rather than merely local damage, and the size at the boundary between airburst and ground impact. There are an estimated **25,000 or so** near-Earth objects at this size or larger. As of the mid-2020s we have found roughly **40 percent** of them.

Sit with that number. It means that of the objects capable of destroying a city or a region, **the majority are still unknown** — their orbits uncomputed, their existence unrecorded. The hazard from this population is not that a tracked object will surprise us; a tracked object with a known orbit is either dangerous, in which case we would have years to act, or safe, in which case we can forget it. **The hazard is dominated entirely by what has not yet been found.** You cannot deflect what you cannot see, and you cannot see what you have not surveyed. This is the single most important sentence in planetary defence, and it is a statement about a *catalogue*, not about rockets.

### The machines that do the looking

The catalogue is filled by a small number of dedicated survey telescopes that photograph the sky repeatedly and software that flags the points of light that move against the fixed stars. The **Catalina Sky Survey** in Arizona and **Pan-STARRS** in Hawaii between them have discovered the majority of known near-Earth objects, sweeping the night sky for the faint moving specks of asteroids. **ATLAS** — the Asteroid Terrestrial-impact Last Alert System — is optimised not for the deep catalogue but for the final warning, scanning the whole visible sky every night or two to catch small objects in the days before a possible impact, exactly the regime a Chelyabinsk-class body would fall into if it came from the night side.

Two coming instruments change the scale of the effort. The **Vera C. Rubin Observatory** in Chile, with its 8.4-metre mirror and a 3,200-megapixel camera, will carry out the Legacy Survey of Space and Time, imaging the entire southern sky every few nights to unprecedented depth; its by-product catalogue of near-Earth objects is expected to be enormous, because a telescope built to find faint galaxies incidentally finds faint asteroids. And **NEO Surveyor**, a NASA space telescope built for this one job, will observe in the **infrared** from the Sun–Earth Lagrange point sunward of us. Infrared is the right choice for two reasons: an asteroid's thermal glow depends on its size and temperature rather than on how reflective it happens to be, so an infrared survey gives cleaner size estimates and does not miss the dark objects an optical survey undercounts; and by stationing itself sunward and looking outward, it can see into part — though not all — of the sunward zone that defeats ground telescopes. NEO Surveyor is the instrument most directly aimed at closing the 140-metre gap, and its schedule is a direct function of its funding.

## A rehearsal with the whole world watching: Apophis, 2029

On the evening of **Friday, 13 April 2029**, an asteroid about 340 to 370 metres across, named **99942 Apophis** for the Egyptian serpent of chaos, will pass closer to the Earth than the ring of geostationary communications satellites. Its closest approach brings it to roughly **38,000 kilometres from the Earth's centre** — inside the geostationary radius of about 42,000 kilometres, and barely a tenth of the distance to the Moon. An object of this size coming this close happens about once every few thousand years. That we will have decades of forewarning, and instruments ready, makes 2029 unique in the history of the subject.

{{image: 99942 Apophis | Radar imagery of the asteroid 99942 Apophis, which on 13 April 2029 will pass inside the ring of geostationary satellites — closer than many spacecraft — and briefly become visible to the naked eye as a moving point of light.}}

When Apophis was discovered in 2004, follow-up briefly suggested a small but genuinely alarming chance of an impact in 2029, and for a short while it held the highest rating any asteroid has reached on the hazard scales. More observations, as almost always happens, shrank the uncertainty ellipse until the Earth fell outside it. **Apophis will not hit the Earth in 2029, nor in 2036, nor at any encounter this century** — radar ranging during a 2021 pass tightened its orbit enough to rule out an impact for at least a hundred years. The chaos in its name is retired. What remains is the experiment.

During the pass, Apophis will brighten to about third magnitude — comparable to the stars of the Little Dipper — and will be visible to the **naked eye** as a fast-moving point of light, tracking across the sky over a few hours from Europe and Africa. It will be the first time in recorded history that a body of this class has been watched, unaided, crossing the sky. It will not show a disc; at 38,000 kilometres a 370-metre object subtends only about two arcseconds, far below what the eye can resolve, so it appears as a moving star.

The scientific prize is subtler than the spectacle. Apophis is almost certainly a **rubble pile** — not a monolithic rock but a loose aggregate of boulders, gravel, and dust held together by their own feeble mutual gravity and a little cohesion, the structure that we now believe describes most asteroids in this size range. As it sweeps past the Earth, the planet's gravity will pull harder on the near side of Apophis than on the far side, and the resulting **tidal stress** will tug at the rubble pile. It will not tear the asteroid apart — the encounter distance is well outside the Roche limit at which tides overwhelm self-gravity, roughly a factor of two farther out than the danger zone. But it may be enough to trigger **landslides, seismic shaking, the resettling of surface boulders, perhaps a small change in spin**. We have never watched tides work on a rubble pile in real time. Apophis in 2029 is a free, full-scale natural experiment in exactly the kind of loosely-bound mechanics that governs how such a body would respond to a deflection attempt. The subject will have a fleet of spacecraft and every large telescope on Earth trained on it, turning a near miss into the best-observed asteroid encounter ever.

## The experiment that has flown: DART, 2022

Everything to this point is observation and inference. On **26 September 2022**, planetary defence acquired something it had never had before: a *result*. The **Double Asteroid Redirection Test** — DART — flew a half-tonne spacecraft into an asteroid on purpose, at 6.14 kilometres per second, to find out whether a kinetic impact could measurably change an asteroid's orbit. It could, and by far more than anyone had a right to expect.

The target was chosen with care. **Didymos** is a near-Earth asteroid about 780 metres across, and it has a small moon, **Dimorphos**, about 160 metres across, orbiting it every 11 hours and 55 minutes. This binary arrangement is what made the experiment possible. Measuring a tiny change in the heliocentric orbit of a lone asteroid — a few centimetres per second out of tens of kilometres per second — would have been hopeless from Earth. But Dimorphos's orbit *around Didymos* is slow, only about 17 centimetres per second, and its 12-hour period could be measured with exquisite precision from the ground: as the moon passes in front of and behind the primary, the combined brightness of the pair dips, and timing those dips over many orbits pins down the period to seconds. Hit the moon, shift its period, and the shift shows up unmistakably in the light curve. **DART turned an unmeasurable heliocentric deflection into an easily measurable change in a binary period** — a piece of experimental design as elegant as the physics it tested.

{{image: Double Asteroid Redirection Test | The last complete image of Dimorphos returned by DART seconds before impact on 26 September 2022, showing the boulder-strewn surface of a rubble-pile asteroid; the collision shortened the moon's orbit by more than half an hour.}}

DART struck Dimorphos head-on against its orbital motion, and stopped transmitting in the same instant — the final images streamed back showed the surface resolving into a jumble of boulders, then a rock filling the frame, then nothing. The prediction, made public beforehand, was that the impact might shorten Dimorphos's orbital period by around seven minutes if the spacecraft simply transferred its own momentum. The measured result, from weeks of careful light-curve timing, was a period change of about **32 minutes** — later refined to 33. The orbit had shrunk far more than the bare momentum of the spacecraft could account for. That overperformance is the entire scientific point of the mission, and understanding it is understanding what a kinetic impactor actually does.

### Why the ejecta did most of the work

A kinetic impactor does not deflect an asteroid simply by embedding itself and handing over its momentum, like a bullet stopping in sand. When the spacecraft hits, it blasts a crater and throws out a spray of rock and dust — **ejecta** — at high speed. That ejecta leaves the asteroid, and by conservation of momentum the asteroid recoils in the opposite direction. The recoil from the ejecta is *in addition to* the momentum the spacecraft delivered, and it can easily dominate. The asteroid is pushed not only by the spacecraft hitting it but by the material rocketing off its own surface.

We capture this with a single dimensionless number, the **momentum enhancement factor**, written $\beta$:

$$\Delta p_{\text{asteroid}} = \beta \, \Delta p_{\text{impactor}}, \qquad \beta = 1 + \frac{p_{\text{ejecta}}}{p_{\text{impactor}}}.$$

If no ejecta escaped, $\beta = 1$ and the asteroid gets exactly the spacecraft's momentum. If the ejecta carries off as much momentum as the spacecraft delivered, $\beta = 2$, and the asteroid is pushed twice as hard as a naive calculation says. For DART, the measured value came out near **$\beta \approx 3.6$** — the recoil from the ejecta plume contributed something like two and a half times the impactor's own momentum. A cubesat companion, LICIACube, flew past minutes later and photographed the plume: a spectacular fan of debris thousands of kilometres long, streaming off Dimorphos like a comet's tail. That plume was the extra push, made visible.

The value of $\beta$ is the real deliverable, and it is worth being clear about why. **$\beta$ is not a property of the spacecraft. It is a property of the asteroid** — specifically of how its surface responds to a hypervelocity impact, which depends on its porosity, its strength, the size distribution of its boulders, how loosely the rubble is bound. A solid rock throws off little ejecta and gives $\beta$ near 1. A loose, porous rubble pile can eject a great deal and give a large $\beta$. So DART did not merely prove that we can hit an asteroid; it measured, for the first time, **how much bonus momentum a real rubble pile gives back**. Any future deflection will be designed around a $\beta$ appropriate to its target, and DART turned that number from a theorist's guess spanning a factor of several into a measured quantity anchored to a real object. The high value is genuine good news: it means a kinetic impactor is several times more effective than its raw momentum implies, provided the target is the loose rubble pile that most such asteroids appear to be.

### The other half of the experiment: Hera

DART left one crucial number unmeasured. To turn the observed period change into a clean value of $\beta$, you need the **mass** of Dimorphos — and DART could only estimate it, because it destroyed itself on arrival and never determined how much material it had actually pushed. The mass entered the $\beta$ calculation as the largest single uncertainty. You also want to see the crater: its size and shape encode how the surface responded, and whether DART left a neat pit or, as some analyses suggest for so weak a body, actually **reshaped the whole moon**.

This is the mission of **Hera**, the European Space Agency spacecraft launched in October 2024 and due to arrive at the Didymos system at the end of 2026. Hera will rendezvous with the pair, orbit them at close range, and measure Dimorphos's mass directly from its gravitational tug, image the DART crater in detail, and characterise the internal structure of a rubble pile as no mission has before. With Hera's mass measurement, the value of $\beta$ will sharpen from "about 3.6" to a precise number, and the whole experiment — impactor plus follow-up survey — will become the calibrated foundation on which a real deflection would be designed. DART fired the shot; Hera reads the target. Only with both is the experiment complete.

## The physics of moving a mountain

Now the reason the whole enterprise is worth the money: the astonishing leverage that time gives you. The governing insight of planetary defence is that **a small nudge applied early beats an enormous shove applied late, by orders of magnitude.** Understanding why turns the problem from one of brute force into one of foresight.

An asteroid heading for Earth and the Earth itself are both orbiting the Sun. To make the asteroid miss, you do not need to shove it sideways out of the way at the last moment. You need only to change its **speed along its orbit** by a tiny amount, well in advance. A small change in orbital velocity slightly changes the orbital period, so the asteroid arrives at the crossing point a little early or a little late — and over many orbits, that timing error accumulates into a growing gap between where the asteroid is and where the Earth is. The asteroid does not so much dodge the Earth as *arrive at the appointment on the wrong day*.

To an order of magnitude, a velocity change $\Delta v$ applied a time $t$ before the encounter opens up a miss distance of roughly

$$d \approx \Delta v \cdot t.$$

The displacement is proportional to the lead time. Double the warning and you halve the required $\Delta v$; give yourself ten times the warning and you need a tenth of the push. Because momentum is what a deflection mission actually delivers, and momentum scales with $\Delta v$, **the cost of a deflection falls in direct proportion to how early you start.**

Put numbers to it. Suppose you must open a miss distance of about two Earth radii, $d \approx 1.3 \times 10^{7}$ m, to turn a certain hit into a clean miss. With **20 years** of warning ($t \approx 6.3 \times 10^{8}$ s):

$$\Delta v \approx \frac{1.3\times10^{7}}{6.3\times10^{8}} \approx 0.02 \ \text{m/s} = 2 \ \text{cm/s}.$$

Two centimetres per second, applied two decades out, is enough. Now suppose the same object is found only **six months** before impact ($t \approx 1.6 \times 10^{7}$ s):

$$\Delta v \approx \frac{1.3\times10^{7}}{1.6\times10^{7}} \approx 0.8 \ \text{m/s}.$$

The required velocity change jumps by a factor of forty, and because the asteroid's mass is unchanged, so does the momentum — and hence the number of impactor spacecraft — you must deliver. A deflection that takes a single modest spacecraft with 20 years' notice takes an implausible armada with six months' notice, if it is possible at all. This is the mathematical heart of the closing argument. **Warning time is not a convenience; it is the resource that makes deflection cheap or impossible.** And warning time is exactly what a complete survey buys. The value of finding an object decades early is not that it lets us worry sooner; it is that it drops the deflection cost by the ratio of the lead times, which is to say by a factor that can reach into the hundreds.

### Three ways to push, and when each applies

The leverage argument tells you to act early. It does not tell you *how* to push. Three techniques span the plausible range, and the choice among them depends on how much warning you have and how big the object is.

**The kinetic impactor** is DART, scaled up: fly a spacecraft into the asteroid and let momentum, amplified by $\beta$, do the work. It is the only method demonstrated in flight, it is mechanically simple, and with adequate warning it handles the great majority of the threatening population — the objects up to a few hundred metres across. Its limitations are that the impulse is delivered all at once and cannot be finely tuned, that it needs a well-characterised target so that $\beta$ is known, and that against a very large object a single impactor delivers too little momentum. For a rubble pile there is also the concern that too violent an impact could fragment the target rather than deflect it intact — trading one hazard for several.

**The gravity tractor** is the opposite temperament: patience made into an instrument. Park a heavy spacecraft near the asteroid and simply hover, station-keeping against the feeble mutual gravitational attraction between the two. That attraction, though minuscule, tugs the asteroid steadily toward the spacecraft; run the tractor for months or years and the accumulated $\Delta v$ can be enough. Its virtues are precision and controllability — the pull is gentle, continuous, and exactly calculable, it works on any composition because it never touches the surface, and it can make the small final correction that steers an asteroid out of a **keyhole**, the narrow patch of space that a resonant return would turn into a later impact. Its vice is that it is extraordinarily weak and therefore demands the most lead time of any method: it is a scalpel for objects found very early, not a hammer for emergencies.

**The nuclear standoff burst** is the option of last resort and largest capacity. Detonate a nuclear device not on the surface but at a calculated distance beside the asteroid; the burst of X-rays and neutrons flash-heats and vaporises a thin layer of the near-side surface, which blows off as ejecta and drives the asteroid the other way — a kinetic impactor's ejecta mechanism, but with the energy delivered by radiation instead of a collision. It is by orders of magnitude the most energetic tool available, the only one with the muscle to move a kilometre-class object or to act on short notice, and for that reason the only credible answer to the two worst cases: a very large object, or any object discovered too late for the gentle methods to have worked. Its costs are real — the political and legal weight of flying nuclear devices into space, and the risk, against a weak rubble pile, of shattering the target into a shotgun blast of smaller but still dangerous fragments. It is the method we hope never to need and would be reckless not to understand.

The unifying thread is lead time. **Gravity tractor for the earliest warnings and the finest adjustments; kinetic impactor for the common case with years to spare; nuclear standoff for the giant or the eleventh hour.** Every one of them works better the sooner it starts — which returns us, once more, to the catalogue.

## The one catastrophe we could cancel

Gather the argument. The impact record is real, written in the iridium at the K-Pg boundary, in the felled forest at Tunguska, in the shattered glass at Chelyabinsk. The size-frequency distribution tells us the extinction-level threat is largely found and, for now, absent, while the city-killer threat at the 140-metre scale is real, present, and **mostly undiscovered** — the residual hazard living entirely in the objects not yet in the catalogue. The physics of deflection is not merely understood but, since DART, *flown*: we know a kinetic impactor works, we have measured the momentum enhancement that makes it several times more effective than its raw momentum, and Hera will soon calibrate that number exactly. And the leverage of early warning is such that finding a threatening object decades ahead reduces the deflection to a task a single modest spacecraft can accomplish, while finding it too late makes deflection difficult or impossible.

Every link in that chain is in hand except one. We can compute orbits to the metre. We can rendezvous with an asteroid, hit it, and measure the result. We can, with adequate warning, push it aside for the cost of one space mission. The **only** thing we cannot presently do is guarantee that we will find the object in time — and that is not a limit of physics or engineering. It is a limit of how many telescopes are looking, how deep they see, and how much of the sunward blind spot we choose to cover. It is the completeness of a survey, and survey completeness is bought with telescope time and instruments, and telescope time and instruments are bought with money.

That is the whole of it, and it is worth stating without hedging. Among all the natural catastrophes that could befall the Earth — the earthquakes we can only prepare for, the eruptions we can only flee, the eventual swelling of the Sun we can only outlive or escape — the asteroid impact stands alone as the one a sufficiently attentive civilisation could **prevent completely**. Not survive; prevent. And the thing standing between us and that unique achievement is not a scientific mystery or a technological gap. It is the pace at which we choose to finish the catalogue. **Planetary defence is the rare existential problem whose solution is fully specified and whose remaining obstacle is a funding decision.** The rocks that killed the dinosaurs never knew they were coming. We would.

## Further reading

The primary sources here are unusually accessible, because so much of this subject is recent and public. NASA's planetary defence program and the DART and Hera mission pages document the impact experiment and its follow-up in plain language and release the data and imagery for anyone to examine; the near-Earth object catalogue and the running tally of survey completeness are published and updated continuously, so the 140-metre completeness figure quoted here is something you can check for yourself and watch improve. For the discovery that started the field as a science rather than a curiosity, Walter Alvarez's own account {{book: Walter Alvarez | T. rex and the Crater of Doom | 1997}} tells the story of the iridium anomaly and the decade-long argument from the inside, by the geologist who found the clay.

- **Walter Alvarez, *T. rex and the Crater of Doom* (1997).** A first-hand narrative of how the K-Pg impact hypothesis was found, resisted, and finally confirmed, written with a working scientist's honesty about the doubt.

## Problems

*Useful constants and figures: stony asteroid density $\rho \approx 3300 \ \text{kg/m}^3$ (use $2600 \ \text{kg/m}^3$ for a porous rubble pile where stated); $1 \ \text{megaton} = 4.18 \times 10^{15}$ J; Earth mass $M_\oplus = 5.97\times10^{24}$ kg, radius $R_\oplus = 6371$ km; $G = 6.674\times10^{-11}$; 1 yr $= 3.16\times10^{7}$ s. DART figures: impactor mass 570 kg, impact speed 6.14 km/s; Dimorphos mass $\approx 4.3\times10^{9}$ kg, orbital speed $\approx 0.17$ m/s, period 11.92 h shortened by 33 min. Problems 1, 2 and 3 are the quantitative core; problem 4 is the reasoning core; problem 9 has no settled answer and is meant to be argued.*

**1 — The cube law, in energy.** **(a)** Compute the impact kinetic energy, in megatons, of a stony sphere 20 m across striking at 19 km/s. Compare it to the observed Chelyabinsk yield of about half a megaton. **(b)** Repeat for a 140 m sphere at the same speed. **(c)** Without recomputing from scratch, explain how you could have got (b) from (a) in one step, and state what that step reveals about why diameter is the master variable. **(d)** The 140 m energy exceeds the largest nuclear weapon ever tested (about 50 Mt). In one or two sentences, say why the 140 m scale — and not, say, the 20 m scale — is the one Congress and the survey community fixed on as the completeness benchmark.

**2 — Reading the recoil: the momentum enhancement factor.** DART's 570 kg spacecraft hit Dimorphos at 6.14 km/s and shortened its orbital period by 33 minutes out of 11.92 hours. Model the orbit as circular with speed 0.17 m/s, and use the near-circular relation $\Delta T / T \approx 3\,\Delta v / v_{\text{orb}}$, where $\Delta v$ is the change in Dimorphos's orbital speed. **(a)** Compute the impactor's momentum. **(b)** From the period change, find $\Delta v$ imparted to Dimorphos, then the total momentum change of Dimorphos using its mass $4.3\times10^{9}$ kg. **(c)** Take the ratio to get $\beta$. **(d)** Explain in words why $\beta$ came out well above 1, and why $\beta$ is properly called a property of the *asteroid* rather than of the spacecraft. **(e)** What single quantity in this calculation was DART least able to pin down, and which mission is being sent to measure it?

**3 — Why early beats late.** An asteroid is on a collision course. To convert the hit into a clean miss you must open a cross-track displacement of two Earth radii, $d \approx 1.3\times10^{7}$ m, using the estimate $d \approx \Delta v \cdot t$ for a small along-track velocity change $\Delta v$ applied a lead time $t$ before encounter. **(a)** Find the required $\Delta v$ for a lead time of 20 years, and again for 6 months. **(b)** The threatening object is 140 m across (rubble-pile density 2600 kg/m³). Compute the momentum $M\,\Delta v$ that a deflection must deliver in each case. **(c)** DART delivered a momentum of order $10^{7}$ kg·m/s including its $\beta$ enhancement. Roughly how many DART-class impacts does each scenario require? **(d)** State, in one sentence, what this comparison implies about the relative value of a dollar spent on surveys versus a dollar spent on deflection hardware.

**4 — The anatomy of a winning argument.** The impact theory of the K-Pg extinction was published in 1980 and not widely accepted for roughly a decade. **(a)** Explain precisely why iridium, of all elements, was the right tracer — what is special about its abundance in the crust and its delivery from space. **(b)** The boundary clay was enriched in iridium about thirtyfold. Why does that specific fact argue for a sudden extraterrestrial delivery rather than slow accumulation or a terrestrial source? **(c)** A global iridium layer was found at many sites, yet the hypothesis was still resisted. What piece of evidence was missing, and when and how was it supplied? **(d)** The initial resistance came largely from geologists committed to uniformitarianism. Argue that this resistance was good scientific practice rather than mere stubbornness.

**5 — The blind spot.** Chelyabinsk (2013) and, plausibly, Tunguska (1908) both arrived essentially undetected. **(a)** Explain the geometric reason a ground-based survey telescope cannot see an object approaching from close to the sunward direction. **(b)** Why does this blind spot make the *warning* problem fundamentally different from the *catalogue* problem — that is, why can a survey that eventually finds 90 percent of 140 m objects still be ambushed by a 20 m one? **(c)** NEO Surveyor observes in the infrared from a position sunward of Earth. Explain both advantages this gives it — one about *which* objects it detects well, one about *where* it can look. **(d)** What kind of future observatory would most directly close the remaining sunward gap, and why can it not be built on the ground?

**6 — Apophis as a laboratory.** On 13 April 2029 the ~370 m asteroid Apophis passes about 38,000 km from the Earth's centre. **(a)** Compare this to the geostationary radius (~42,000 km) and the Earth–Moon distance (~384,000 km), and state what makes the pass historically unusual. **(b)** Estimate the angular size of Apophis at closest approach and compare it to the eye's resolution (about 60 arcseconds); what will an observer actually see? **(c)** Treat Apophis as a rubble pile of density 2600 kg/m³. Compare the tidal acceleration the Earth raises across it, $a_{\text{tide}} \approx 2GM_\oplus r / d^3$, with the asteroid's own surface self-gravity, $g_{\text{self}} = \tfrac{4}{3}\pi G \rho r$, and interpret the ratio. **(d)** Given that ratio, will Apophis be torn apart? What, more subtly, might the encounter still do to it, and why is that scientifically valuable?

**7 — Two flavours of impact.** Tunguska (10–15 Mt) left no crater and no recovered fragment, while Chicxulub (~10⁸ Mt) left a 180 km crater. **(a)** Explain physically why a 50–60 m stony object detonates in the air rather than reaching the ground intact, and why a 10 km object does not. **(b)** Chelyabinsk injured about 1,500 people, almost all by broken glass rather than by anything striking them. Reconstruct the sequence of events that produced that casualty pattern, and identify the human behaviour that made it worse. **(c)** For the same total energy, argue whether an airburst or a ground impact is more dangerous to a nearby population — and note why the answer depends on altitude.

**8 — Choosing the tool.** For each scenario, name the most appropriate deflection method (kinetic impactor, gravity tractor, or nuclear standoff burst) and justify the choice in one or two sentences. **(a)** A 150 m rubble pile found 25 years before a possible impact, whose orbit passes through a gravitational keyhole in 2041 that would set up a strike in 2048. **(b)** A 400 m object found 30 years out, well characterised, on a certain-impact trajectory. **(c)** A 900 m object discovered only 4 years before impact. **(d)** Explain why every one of these answers would shift toward the gentler, cheaper end of the list if the survey that found the object had been completed a decade sooner.

**9 — Open problem: how complete is complete enough?** Congress set a goal of cataloguing 90 percent of near-Earth objects 140 m and larger; roughly 40 percent are currently found, and the fraction climbs slowly. **(a)** Why 140 m and 90 percent — what is the logic of each number, and what does 90 rather than 100 percent concede? **(b)** The residual risk after a survey is dominated by the objects still missing. Sketch how you would quantify the *expected casualties per year* still outstanding as a function of survey completeness, and argue whether that risk falls linearly, faster, or slower as completeness rises toward 90 percent. **(c)** Planetary defence competes for funding with every other public good. Construct the strongest case that accelerating NEO Surveyor and Rubin is a rational allocation, and then the strongest case against — and say which you find more persuasive and why. **(d)** Suppose a complete 140 m survey is finished and finds no threatening object for the next century. Was the money wasted? Defend your answer.

## Worked answers

**1 — The cube law, in energy.** **(a)** Radius 10 m, so volume $\tfrac{4}{3}\pi(10)^3 = 4.19\times10^{3} \ \text{m}^3$ and mass $m = 3300 \times 4.19\times10^3 = 1.38\times10^{7}$ kg. Then $E = \tfrac{1}{2}(1.38\times10^7)(1.9\times10^4)^2 = \tfrac{1}{2}(1.38\times10^7)(3.61\times10^8) = 2.49\times10^{15}$ J. Dividing by $4.18\times10^{15}$ J/Mt gives **about 0.6 Mt** — within a factor of two of the observed half-megaton Chelyabinsk yield, which is as close as this crude model has any right to be. **(b)** Radius 70 m, volume $\tfrac{4}{3}\pi(70)^3 = 1.44\times10^{6} \ \text{m}^3$, mass $4.74\times10^{9}$ kg, and $E = \tfrac{1}{2}(4.74\times10^9)(1.9\times10^4)^2 = 8.6\times10^{17}$ J $= $ **about 205 Mt**. **(c)** In one step: the diameter grew by a factor of 7, and energy scales as mass scales as the *cube* of diameter, so multiply (a) by $7^3 = 343$: $0.6 \times 343 \approx 205$ Mt. That is the whole lesson — a modest increase in size is a huge increase in energy, so the destructive potential of the population is concentrated in its largest members, and the survey question is dominated by the biggest objects still unfound. **(d)** The 20 m scale does regional-glass damage at worst and its objects are too numerous to catalogue completely; the 140 m scale is the smallest that reliably delivers hundreds of megatons — regional devastation rather than a bad day — while still being rare enough (tens of thousands of objects) that finding nearly all of them is a finite, fundable task. It is the sweet spot where the damage is civilisation-relevant and the catalogue is achievable.

**2 — The momentum enhancement factor.** **(a)** Impactor momentum $p = m v = 570 \times 6140 = 3.5\times10^{6}$ kg·m/s. **(b)** Period change fraction $\Delta T/T = 33/(11.92\times60) = 33/715 = 0.0462$. Then $\Delta v = v_{\text{orb}}\,\Delta T/(3T) = 0.17 \times 0.0462/3 = 2.6\times10^{-3}$ m/s — a couple of millimetres per second. Dimorphos's momentum change is $M\,\Delta v = 4.3\times10^{9} \times 2.6\times10^{-3} = 1.1\times10^{7}$ kg·m/s. **(c)** $\beta = (1.1\times10^7)/(3.5\times10^6) \approx 3.2$. The idealised tangential model lands a little short of the officially reported $\beta \approx 3.6$; the difference comes from the impact not being perfectly along-track and from the uncertain mass — but the qualitative result is unmistakable and robust: $\beta$ is several times unity. **(d)** $\beta$ exceeds 1 because the impact excavated a crater and blasted out a plume of ejecta; that ejecta carried away momentum, and the asteroid recoiled against it, adding to the spacecraft's own push. The size of that bonus depends on how much material a hypervelocity impact throws off, which is governed by the target's porosity, strength, and boulder structure — properties of the *asteroid*, not the projectile. A solid rock would give $\beta$ near 1; a loose rubble pile gives a large $\beta$, and Dimorphos is evidently the latter. **(e)** The mass of Dimorphos was the least-constrained quantity — DART destroyed itself and could only estimate how much material it had pushed, so the mass dominated the error in $\beta$. **Hera**, the ESA follow-up launched in 2024, will measure that mass directly from the asteroid's gravity, sharpening $\beta$ to a precise, calibrated value.

**3 — Why early beats late.** **(a)** With $d = 1.3\times10^7$ m and $d \approx \Delta v \cdot t$: for $t = 20$ yr $= 6.3\times10^8$ s, $\Delta v = 1.3\times10^7/6.3\times10^8 = 0.021$ m/s $\approx$ **2 cm/s**; for $t = 6$ months $= 1.6\times10^7$ s, $\Delta v = 1.3\times10^7/1.6\times10^7 = 0.81$ m/s $\approx$ **0.8 m/s**. The late case demands about **40 times** the velocity change. **(b)** A 140 m rubble pile of density 2600 has mass $m = 2600 \times \tfrac{4}{3}\pi(70)^3 = 2600 \times 1.44\times10^6 = 3.7\times10^{9}$ kg. Momentum required: early, $M\,\Delta v = 3.7\times10^9 \times 0.021 = 7.9\times10^{7}$ kg·m/s; late, $3.7\times10^9 \times 0.81 = 3.0\times10^{9}$ kg·m/s. **(c)** Dividing by DART's delivered $\sim10^7$ kg·m/s: the early case needs roughly **8 DART-class impacts**, the late case roughly **300** — the latter an implausible campaign that likely could not be mounted or flown in six months at all. **(d)** A dollar spent on surveys buys warning time, and warning time cuts the deflection momentum in direct proportion, so early detection is the cheapest possible form of deflection — the survey dollar is worth far more than the hardware dollar because it multiplies the effectiveness of every hardware dollar that might follow.

**4 — The anatomy of a winning argument.** **(a)** Iridium is a siderophile: when the young Earth melted and differentiated, almost all of its iridium followed the iron into the core, leaving the crust extraordinarily poor in it. Meanwhile iridium arrives steadily from space in micrometeorite dust, and asteroids (undifferentiated, or made of core-like material) are rich in it. So crustal iridium is a near-pure signal of extraterrestrial input against an almost blank background — a rare element on Earth that is common in what hits the Earth. **(b)** A thirtyfold spike concentrated in a thin layer cannot come from the slow cosmic-dust rain, which would smear a small enrichment over a great thickness; nor is there a terrestrial reservoir to draw such a quantity from, since the crust is depleted. A large, sharp spike therefore points to a single sudden delivery of iridium-rich extraterrestrial material — a big impact. **(c)** The missing piece was the **crater** itself: a global chemical layer proves a global event but not its mechanism. It was supplied a decade later, in 1991, when the buried 180 km Chicxulub structure beneath the Yucatán — originally mapped by oil prospectors and dismissed as volcanic — was reidentified as an impact crater and dated to 66 Myr, matching the boundary exactly, with shocked quartz and tektites confirming it. Prediction first, physical confirmation second. **(d)** Geology had spent a century replacing biblical catastrophism with uniformitarianism, and that shift was a genuine intellectual achievement that made the field quantitative. A single chemical anomaly asserting a sudden global cataclysm was exactly the kind of claim that had misled the field before, so demanding a crater — a second, independent, physical line of evidence — before overturning the reigning framework was disciplined skepticism working as it should. The hypothesis was accepted when, and because, it met that standard.

**5 — The blind spot.** **(a)** A survey telescope detects an asteroid by the sunlight it reflects, against a dark sky. That requires pointing away from the Sun, into the night side; an object approaching from within the cone of sky centred on the Sun is up only in daytime, lost in the overwhelming glare of the scattered solar light, and cannot be picked out. **(b)** The catalogue problem is about the *large* objects, which move slowly enough on the sky and are bright enough that repeated all-sky surveys eventually catch them on the night side over months and years, wherever they came from. The warning problem is about *small* objects on their final approach, which may only become detectable days ahead — and if those final days place them in the sunward cone, no amount of catalogue completeness helps, because they were never bright enough to log earlier and are invisible now. A 20 m body like Chelyabinsk is below the catalogue threshold and can arrive entirely inside the blind spot. **(c)** Infrared gives two advantages: first, an asteroid's thermal emission depends on its size and temperature rather than its reflectivity, so an infrared survey detects dark objects that an optical survey misses and yields cleaner sizes — it does not undercount the low-albedo population. Second, by stationing itself sunward of Earth and looking outward and to the sides, it can survey much of the region near the Earth's orbit, including part of the zone that is lost in daylight from the ground. **(d)** The remaining gap — objects approaching from almost directly sunward — would be best closed by a space telescope placed **interior to Earth's orbit**, near Venus's distance, looking outward past the Earth; from there the dangerous sunward approaches are seen against a dark sky. It cannot be done from the ground because no ground telescope can escape the daytime sky; the geometry requires leaving Earth entirely.

**6 — Apophis as a laboratory.** **(a)** At 38,000 km from Earth's centre, Apophis passes *inside* the ~42,000 km geostationary radius — closer than our communications satellites — and at only about a tenth of the 384,000 km lunar distance. An object several hundred metres across coming this close happens roughly once in several thousand years, and never before with instruments ready and decades of forewarning; that combination is what makes it unique. **(b)** Angular size $\theta \approx D/d = 370/(3.8\times10^7) = 9.7\times10^{-6}$ rad $= 2.0$ arcseconds, far below the eye's ~60 arcsecond resolution. So the eye sees no disc — only a **star-like point of about third magnitude moving visibly across the sky** over a few hours. **(c)** Take $r = 185$ m, $d = 3.8\times10^7$ m. Tidal: $a_{\text{tide}} = 2GM_\oplus r/d^3 = 2(6.674\times10^{-11})(5.97\times10^{24})(185)/(3.8\times10^7)^3 = 1.47\times10^{17}/5.5\times10^{22} = 2.7\times10^{-6} \ \text{m/s}^2$. Self-gravity: $g_{\text{self}} = \tfrac{4}{3}\pi G\rho r = \tfrac{4}{3}\pi(6.674\times10^{-11})(2600)(185) = 1.34\times10^{-4} \ \text{m/s}^2$. Ratio $a_{\text{tide}}/g_{\text{self}} \approx 0.02$ — the tide is about **2 percent** of the body's own gravity. **(d)** No: disruption requires the tide to approach or exceed self-gravity (the Roche condition), and at 2 percent the pass is comfortably outside the Roche limit, roughly a factor of two farther out than the danger zone. But 2 percent is not nothing on a loosely bound rubble pile: the encounter could trigger landslides, seismic shaking, boulder resettling, or a small spin change. That is valuable precisely because we have never watched tides act on a rubble pile in real time, and how such a body deforms under stress is the same mechanics that governs how it would respond to a deflection.

**7 — Two flavours of impact.** **(a)** As an object plunges through the atmosphere it feels a ram pressure $\sim \rho_{\text{air}} v^2$ that rises steeply as the air thickens. A 50–60 m stone reaches a depth where that pressure exceeds its own modest material strength while it is still kilometres up; it fragments, the fragments present more area, decelerate violently, and dump their energy into the air almost at once — an airburst, no crater. A 10 km object carries so much momentum per unit area that it crosses the entire atmosphere in a couple of seconds, essentially undecelerated, and delivers its energy into the ground, excavating a crater. Strength matters at small sizes; sheer mass overwhelms the atmosphere at large ones. **(b)** Sequence: the object burst high over Chelyabinsk in a flash brighter than the Sun; people throughout the city, seeing the extraordinary light, went to their windows to look; tens of seconds later — the light travelled instantly, the blast wave at the speed of sound — the pressure wave reached the ground and shattered windows across the city; the flying glass struck the people who were standing at those windows. The worsening behaviour was the instinct to run *toward* the spectacle and stand at the glass in the interval between seeing the flash and the arrival of the wave. **(c)** For a fixed total energy, an airburst at optimal altitude can actually maximise the *area* over which the blast overpressure is destructive at ground level (this is why airbursts are used for weapons), so for a population spread over a region an airburst can be more dangerous than a ground burst, which craters and cratering wastes energy downward into the earth. But it depends on altitude: burst too high and the wave weakens before it reaches the ground (Chelyabinsk, at ~30 km, injured but did not level the city); burst too low or hit the ground and the damage concentrates. The worst case is an airburst at the altitude that puts the strongest overpressure across the widest populated area.

**8 — Choosing the tool.** **(a)** **Gravity tractor.** With 25 years of lead time and a keyhole to thread, the task is a small, exquisitely precise correction rather than a large shove; the tractor's gentle, continuous, exactly-calculable pull is ideal for nudging the object off the resonant keyhole without risk of fragmenting a rubble pile. **(b)** **Kinetic impactor.** A 400 m well-characterised object with 30 years' warning is squarely in the demonstrated regime; a single impactor (or a few), with $\beta$ known from the target's characterisation, delivers ample momentum with three decades of leverage, and it is the simplest proven method. **(c)** **Nuclear standoff burst.** A 900 m object with only 4 years' warning is both too massive for a kinetic impactor to move enough and too imminent for any gentle method to accumulate the needed $\Delta v$; only the standoff burst has the energy to act at short notice on so large a body — accepting the fragmentation risk because nothing weaker can work. **(d)** Every answer shifts gentler and cheaper with more lead time because the required $\Delta v$, and hence the required force, falls in proportion to the warning: the object in (c) that needs a nuclear device with 4 years' notice might need only a kinetic impactor, or even a tractor, with 14 — so the survey that finds objects a decade earlier is, in effect, what lets us use the safe tools instead of the dangerous ones.

**9 — Open problem: how complete is complete enough?** **(a)** 140 m is the smallest scale that reliably causes regional (not merely local) devastation — hundreds of megatons — while being rare enough that a near-complete catalogue is a finite task; below it the objects are too numerous and individually less catastrophic, above it they are already nearly all found. 90 percent rather than 100 is a deliberate concession that the last few percent — the faint, the dark, the sunward, the awkwardly-orbiting — cost disproportionately more to find than they buy in risk reduction, so the goal is set where the marginal telescope-hour still pays. **(b)** Weight each unfound object by its impact probability per year and its expected casualties, and sum; expected annual casualties outstanding is (population not yet found) × (per-object annual impact probability) × (casualties per impact). Whether it falls linearly or faster depends on *which* objects the survey finds first: surveys preferentially catch the largest and closest-approaching objects early, and those carry disproportionate risk, so the outstanding risk should fall **faster than linearly** at first — the initial 40 percent found removes more than 40 percent of the risk — and then flatten as only the small, faint, hard-to-find residue remains. The policy implication is that early survey progress is especially valuable. **(c)** For: the expected-value cost of an unprevented city-killer impact is enormous, the annual probability though small is nonzero, and the survey is cheap relative to that expected loss and relative to most public safety spending — it is insurance against a catastrophe that is uniquely preventable, and delay only raises the chance of being ambushed. Against: the annual probability is genuinely tiny, the money has certain immediate returns elsewhere (disease, poverty) against a speculative distant one, and a partial survey already retired the extinction-level threat, so the marginal city-killer search has diminishing returns. Which is more persuasive is a value judgement, but the asymmetry — a modest, bounded cost against an unbounded, uniquely *cancellable* catastrophe — makes the case for funding strong on any reasonable expected-value accounting. **(d)** Not wasted. A completed survey that finds no threat has *purchased the knowledge that there is no threat* — it converts an unquantified fear into a measured all-clear, which is exactly what a survey is for. Insurance that pays out nothing because the disaster did not occur was not thereby a bad purchase; the value was in removing the uncertainty, and a null result from a complete catalogue is the best outcome the money could possibly buy.$astroNEO_master$,
    true,
    true
  )
