where node_id in (select node_id from master)
  and not (depth = 'research' and level = 'advanced' and format = 'read');

with q as (
  select $astroNEO_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The Alvarez team inferred a giant impact at the K-Pg boundary from an iridium measurement. What did they actually find, and why was it decisive?", "options": ["The boundary clay was almost completely depleted of iridium, showing that the extinction interval had been too brief for any cosmic dust to accumulate at all", "The boundary clay was enriched in iridium by a factor of about thirty over the rock above and below, a spike far too large and sharp for any plausible slow rain of cosmic dust to produce, implying a single sudden delivery of an object several kilometres across", "The boundary clay contained iridium at exactly the background crustal level, which ruled out volcanism and left an impact as the only source", "The boundary clay showed iridium roughly doubled, a modest enrichment consistent with either a slow deposition or a small local meteorite"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Iridium is a siderophile: when the Earth differentiated, almost all of it sank with the iron into the core, leaving the crust nearly barren. Because space delivers iridium at a roughly constant rate via micrometeorite dust, the Alvarez team expected to use its concentration as a clock for how long the clay took to form. Instead the clay was enriched about thirtyfold, a spike so large and sharp that no slow cosmic-dust rain over any plausible interval could account for it. That forced the conclusion that the iridium arrived all at once, in a quantity implying an extraterrestrial body several kilometres across. Depletion (option 0) or background levels (option 2) would have signalled the opposite of an impact, and a mere doubling (option 3) could have been explained gradually; only the thirtyfold enrichment demanded a sudden extraterrestrial source."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why was the 2013 Chelyabinsk object never seen coming, and what does that reveal about our surveillance?", "options": ["It approached the Earth from a direction only a little more than twenty degrees from the Sun, out of the daytime sky, where ground-based survey telescopes cannot look because they must point into the dark night side to detect reflected sunlight, leaving a permanent sunward blind spot", "At only twenty metres across it was simply too small for any existing telescope to register, regardless of the direction it came from", "The surveys had all been pointed at the southern sky that night, and Chelyabinsk arrived over the northern hemisphere where no telescope happened to be watching", "Its surface was unusually dark and non-reflective, so even though telescopes were pointed at it, it returned too little light to be flagged as a moving object"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "The Chelyabinsk body, about twenty metres across, entered the atmosphere at 19 km/s and burst near thirty kilometres altitude with an energy near half a megaton, injuring about 1,500 people, overwhelmingly by flying glass. The detail that should keep survey astronomers awake is that it was seen by no telescope, not hours or even minutes ahead. The reason is geometric and, from the ground, unfixable: it approached from only about twenty degrees from the Sun, out of the daytime sky. Every ground survey telescope must look at the night side, away from the Sun, because that is where the sky is dark enough to pick out faint reflected sunlight; anything bearing down from the sunward direction is lost in glare until it lights itself up in the atmosphere. Size alone (option 1) is not the barrier, nor a scheduling accident (option 2) or low albedo (option 3): the blind spot is a fixed wedge of sky centred on the Sun, and both Tunguska and Chelyabinsk were, in effect, ambushes from it."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "As of the mid-2020s, where does the impact hazard actually reside, according to the chapter's size-frequency argument?", "options": ["In the kilometre-and-up objects, of which we have found less than half, any one of which could still deliver a mass-extinction blow without warning", "Evenly across all size classes, since the cube law means every object from four metres to ten kilometres poses a comparable expected risk", "In the 140-metre 'city-killer' class: an estimated 25,000 such objects exist, only about 40 percent are found, so the majority remain uncatalogued, and because you cannot deflect what you cannot see, the hazard is dominated entirely by what has not yet been found", "In the tracked 140-metre objects whose orbits are known, because a catalogued object on a resonant return is the hardest kind to deflect in time"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "The kilometre-and-up class is the easiest to see because it is large and bright; decades of survey work have found an estimated 95 percent or more of them and none of the known ones is on a collision course, so the extinction-level threat is retired for now (making option 0 wrong). The residual hazard has migrated downward to 140 metres, the 'city-killer' scale, the smallest size that reliably does regional rather than local damage and the boundary between airburst and ground impact. Of an estimated 25,000 such objects, only about 40 percent are found, so the majority are still unknown. The chapter's single most important sentence is that the hazard is dominated entirely by what has not yet been found: a tracked object is either known-dangerous (giving us years to act) or known-safe, whereas an uncatalogued one can surprise us. That inverts option 3, and the cube law (mass and energy scaling as diameter cubed) makes the risk lopsided toward the largest unfound bodies, not uniform (option 1)."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "DART shortened Dimorphos's orbital period by about 32 minutes, far more than the spacecraft's own momentum could account for. What does the momentum enhancement factor beta (approximately 3.6) actually tell us?", "options": ["That the DART spacecraft was more massive or faster than reported, because only extra impactor momentum could triple the expected effect", "That Dimorphos happened to be much lighter than predicted, so the same push moved it further than the seven-minute forecast", "That the impact excavated a crater and blasted out a plume of ejecta whose recoil pushed the asteroid in addition to the spacecraft's momentum; beta is a property of the asteroid's rubble-pile mechanics (porosity, strength, boulder structure), not of the spacecraft, and its high value means a kinetic impactor is several times more effective against a loose rubble pile than its raw momentum implies", "That the light-curve timing was biased, since a genuine period change from a 570 kg impactor could not physically exceed the seven-minute prediction"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "A kinetic impactor does not merely embed itself and hand over its momentum like a bullet in sand. When DART struck Dimorphos at 6.14 km/s it blasted a crater and threw out ejecta at high speed; by conservation of momentum the asteroid recoiled against that departing material, so the total push was the spacecraft's momentum plus the ejecta recoil. This is captured by beta = 1 + p_ejecta/p_impactor. The pre-impact prediction of about seven minutes assumed pure momentum transfer (beta = 1); the measured 32-minute change (later refined to 33) implied beta near 3.6, meaning the ejecta plume contributed roughly two and a half times the impactor's own momentum, exactly the fan of debris LICIACube photographed streaming off like a comet's tail. Crucially, beta is not a property of the spacecraft but of the asteroid: how much ejecta a hypervelocity impact throws off depends on the target's porosity, strength, and boulder structure. A solid rock gives beta near 1; a loose rubble pile gives a large beta. Options 0, 1, and 3 all misattribute the overperformance to the spacecraft, the mass, or a measurement artefact, missing that the ejecta and the target's rubble-pile mechanics are the whole point."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Using the chapter's estimate d ~ delta-v times t for the miss distance opened by an along-track velocity change, why does finding a threatening object early matter so decisively?", "options": ["Because the displacement is proportional to lead time, so the required delta-v (and hence the deliverable momentum and number of impactor spacecraft) falls in direct proportion to how early you start: about 2 cm/s suffices with 20 years' warning, but roughly 0.8 m/s, forty times more, is needed with only six months, turning a job for one modest spacecraft into an implausible armada", "Because a deflection applied early can shove the asteroid sideways out of Earth's path entirely, whereas a late deflection can only change its arrival time and so is intrinsically weaker", "Because early warning lets engineers build a larger, heavier spacecraft, and the deflection improves with spacecraft mass rather than with how much lead time is available", "Because the required delta-v is roughly the same regardless of timing, but starting early simply gives mission planners more calendar time to launch and cross-check their work"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "The asteroid and the Earth both orbit the Sun, so you need not shove the asteroid sideways at the last moment; you change its speed along its orbit by a tiny amount well in advance, which shifts its period so it arrives at the crossing point early or late, and over many orbits that timing error grows into a miss. To order of magnitude the opened miss distance is d ~ delta-v times t, proportional to lead time, so double the warning and you halve the required delta-v. Using d ~ 1.3 x 10^7 m (about two Earth radii): 20 years (about 6.3 x 10^8 s) needs delta-v ~ 0.02 m/s = 2 cm/s, while six months (about 1.6 x 10^7 s) needs ~0.8 m/s, a factor of forty greater. Because the asteroid's mass is fixed, the momentum, and hence the number of impactor spacecraft, jumps by the same factor. Option 1 misdescribes the mechanism (deflection works by changing arrival timing, not by a last-moment sideways dodge); options 2 and 3 wrongly make the deflection about spacecraft mass or calendar convenience rather than the delta-v-times-time leverage that makes warning time the resource that renders deflection cheap or impossible."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "The chapter's closing thesis is that an asteroid impact is unique among natural catastrophes. State that thesis precisely, explain what makes an impact different from earthquakes, hurricanes, supervolcanoes, or the death of the Sun, and identify exactly what the remaining obstacle is and why the chapter insists it is not a scientific or engineering problem.", "rubric": "A strong answer states the thesis: an asteroid impact is the one natural catastrophe large enough to end a civilisation that a civilisation could, in principle, prevent completely, not merely survive. It should explain the decisive difference: an asteroid obeys Newtonian mechanics exactly, so its trajectory can be computed years ahead with clock-like precision, and a nudge of a few centimetres per second applied early enough turns a hit into a miss. Against every other catastrophe (earthquakes, hurricanes, supervolcanoes, the eventual swelling of the Sun) the only human responses are to build stronger, warn earlier, flee, or endure; none can be cancelled. A strong answer then ties together the supporting chain: the impact record is real (iridium at the K-Pg boundary, the felled forest at Tunguska, the shattered glass at Chelyabinsk); the extinction-level (kilometre-plus) threat is about 95 percent found and currently absent; the residual city-killer hazard at 140 metres is real, present, and mostly undiscovered (only about 40 percent of an estimated 25,000 found); the deflection physics is not merely understood but flown, since DART demonstrated a kinetic impactor works with a measured momentum enhancement (beta about 3.6) and Hera will calibrate it; and early warning gives orders-of-magnitude leverage (delta-v falling in proportion to lead time). The essay should conclude by naming the single remaining obstacle: not physics or engineering but the completeness of the catalogue, the list of what is out there and where it is going, including coverage of the sunward blind spot. Because survey completeness is bought with telescope time and instruments, which are bought with money, the remaining obstacle is a funding decision, a line item in a budget. The best answers capture the chapter's exact framing: planetary defence is the rare existential problem whose solution is fully specified and whose only remaining obstacle is a funding decision, and note the closing contrast that the dinosaurs never knew their impactor was coming, whereas we would."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Contrast the three impact events the chapter uses (Chicxulub, Tunguska 1908, Chelyabinsk 2013) as evidence, explaining what physical signature each left, why Tunguska left no crater while Chicxulub did, and what makes Chelyabinsk the chapter's proof that the hazard is present-tense. Then explain, separately, why Apophis's 13 April 2029 pass is scientifically valuable even though it will not hit the Earth.", "rubric": "A strong answer treats the three impacts as a spread across the size-frequency distribution. Chicxulub is the catastrophic tail: a roughly ten-kilometre object, about a once-in-a-hundred-million-years event, that carried so much energy (about 10^8 megatons) and momentum per unit area that it crossed the atmosphere essentially undecelerated and excavated a 180-kilometre crater beneath the Yucatan, found a full decade after the iridium anomaly predicted it, and dated to 66 million years matching the boundary clay, with shocked quartz, tektites, global soot, and a tsunami deposit confirming it. Tunguska (30 June 1908) was a stony body perhaps fifty to sixty metres across releasing ten to fifteen megatons that left no crater and no recovered fragment: moving fast enough that atmospheric ram pressure (which scales as air density times velocity squared) exceeded its own material strength while still kilometres up, it shattered and dumped its energy into the air almost instantaneously, an airburst that flattened about eighty million trees over some 2,150 square kilometres in a radial butterfly pattern but punched no hole. The key physical contrast: strength matters at small sizes (a few tens of metres detonate overhead), while sheer mass overwhelms the atmosphere at large sizes (ten kilometres reaches the ground). Chelyabinsk (15 February 2013) is the present-tense proof: a roughly twenty-metre body at 19 km/s bursting near thirty kilometres with about half a megaton, injuring about 1,500 people, overwhelmingly by flying glass because they ran to their windows to watch the flash and the shock wave arrived tens of seconds later while they stood at the glass; and, decisively, it was completely undetected because it came from the sunward blind spot, more than twenty degrees from the Sun out of the daytime sky. On Apophis: a roughly 340 to 370 metre asteroid will pass about 38,000 km from Earth's centre on 13 April 2029, inside the geostationary radius (about 42,000 km) and about a tenth of the lunar distance, an event of this size and closeness happening only once in several thousand years, uniquely with decades of forewarning and instruments ready. It will not hit (radar ranging in 2021 ruled out impact for at least a century, and it will not hit in 2029, 2036, or any encounter this century). Its scientific value is that Apophis is almost certainly a rubble pile, and Earth's tidal stress during the pass, though only about two percent of the body's self-gravity (well outside the Roche limit, so no disruption), may trigger landslides, seismic shaking, boulder resettling, or a small spin change. Watching tides act on a rubble pile in real time is a free full-scale experiment in the same loosely-bound mechanics that governs how such a body would respond to a deflection attempt, making it directly relevant to planetary defence. The best answers connect Tunguska and Chelyabinsk as ambushes that motivate the survey argument and connect Apophis's rubble-pile mechanics to DART's beta and the design of real deflections."}]$astroNEO_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/solar-system/small-bodies/near-earth-objects', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
